WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.520
SPEAKER: Google Compute Engine
enables you to deploy updates

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.150
to virtual machine instances in
managed instance groups using

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.510
two different methods.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.810
One, rolling update
incrementally

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.660
deploys updates in all
the VMs of the image.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.420
Two, canary deployment
deploys updates

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.640
to a subset of VMs in the image.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.790
This enables you to test
the update on some VMs

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.250
before the update is
deployed on all the VMs.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.830
In this video, we will
focus on how to perform

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.970
rolling update to MIGs.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.270
MIGs support two
types of update modes.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.880
First, automatic or proactive.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.460
In this mode, you can roll
out new configurations

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.780
to all or to a subset of the
instances automatically in MIG.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.120
Here are some advantages
of proactive mode.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.280
No manual intervention
is required.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.260
You can automate
rollouts partially

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.440
with reactive mode, which
allows you to perform testing

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.060
similar to canary update.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.410
Here is a quick tip.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.150
Choose automatic
mode when you want

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.790
to deploy a critical update
across instances immediately.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.150
Second, selective
or opportunistic.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.780
You can manually start the
update on selected instances

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.330
or when a new
instance are created.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.580
Now, some advantages
of selective mode.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.010
You will have a better
control on the timing

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.810
and the sequence of the update.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.560
And here's some tip for you.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.110
Choose selective
mode when you want

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.050
to perform a pre-planned update
during a maintenance period,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.870
so that the impacts, such
as performance degradation

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.730
or downtime, can
be avoided for you.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.420
Now, let's look at how
to perform a rolling

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.130
update in proactive mode.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:40.230
In your Google Cloud console, go
to Compute Engine and Instance

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.210
Groups.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.960
In our project, there are two
instance groups, rolling update

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.420
proactive mode, rolling
update opportunistic mode.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.620
Each of these MIGs
have three instances.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.230
Other than that, we
also have two templates

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.860
which we use to create
and update the MIGs.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.880
Application version V1, this is
the current version deployed.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.970
Application version V2,
this is the new version

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.289
we want to upgrade
the VMs in our MIG.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:13.260
Select the MIG that
you want to update.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.920
In this example, we will update
rolling update proactive mode

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.240
MIG.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.330
Click Update VMs.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.750
Under New template,
click the dropdown list

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.630
to select the new
template to update to.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.600
We will select
application version V2.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.380
Under Update Configuration,
expand the selection menu.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.970
Select automatic as the update
type to trigger proactive mode.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.810
With proactive mode, you can
control the disruption level

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.290
during rolling update by
selecting the appropriate value

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.640
from the dropdown list in
action allowed to update VMs.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.630
With each option, there are
different instance properties

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:55.800
which will be updated.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.040
Select the option that
works for your requirement.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.880
We will select Replace, which
replaces the current instances

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.130
with new instance.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.590
Next one is maximum surge.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.440
It indicates the
number of VMs that you

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:12.660
want to create above
the target size

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.060
before deleting
the old instances.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.880
Note, the additional
instances will temporarily

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.000
run at the same time as
the instances it replaces.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.400
Setting up a higher
value for maximum surge

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.140
can improve the
speed of the update.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.350
However, it will incur cost
for the additional instances

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.310
created.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.720
Max unavailable.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.670
It indicates the
number of instances

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:36.030
taken offline or unavailable
during the update time.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.500
Note, max unavailable value
can cause downtime on our MIG

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.250
if the value is close or equal
to the number of instances

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.330
in the MIG.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.090
The default value for max
surge and max unavailable

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.570
is 1 for zonal MIG and
3 for regional MIG.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.750
Let's proceed with the default
value of 1 for max surge

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.520
and max unavailable.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.640
Next, in advanced configuration,
we have minimum wait time.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.180
This value sets
the length of time

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.810
to wait between two
successive instance updates.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:07.960
All right.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.250
We are all set.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.660
Now click on Update VMs.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.269
We can open the instance
group in another window

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:19.600
to view the instances
being stopped and getting

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.430
replaced by the new instances
with the updated application

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.930
version.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.870
All right.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:31.000
Let's now look at how to
apply a selective update.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.280
In our project, select
the MIG, rolling

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.020
update opportunistic mode.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.770
Select Update VMs.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.310
Under New Template, select
Application version V2.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.650
Select the option
of selective mode

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.720
under update configuration.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.280
Select Done.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.400
Click Update VMs.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.500
Now, click on the
Instance Group.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.520
Select one of the instance,
and proceed for deletion.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.220
There you go.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.270
We can see that a new VM has
spawned up as a part of the MIG

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.470
with the new version
of the application.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:14.500
Note that, because we have
chosen selective mode,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.990
the other two VMs will not
be updated automatically.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.180
Check out the
documentation to learn more

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.520
about the proactive
and selective modes

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:24.960
in rolling update.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

